.TH CRON\-TRANSLATE 1 "2026-09" "cron-translate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cron-translate \- cron expressions in human terms
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cron-translate
.I EXPRESSION
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Explains a five-field cron expression in words, lists its next runs in a
chosen timezone, and warns where daylight-saving transitions will skip or
repeat one.
.PP
Quote the expression: most of them contain characters the shell would eat.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-tz " ZONE"
IANA timezone for the next runs (default UTC).
.TP
.BI \-\-next " N"
How many next runs to show (default 3).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dst\-check
Skip the daylight-saving warnings.
.TP
.BI \-\-between " START END"
List every run in the closed interval, ISO 8601, e.g.
.BR 2026-07-15T00:00 .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The expression was understood.
.TP
.B non-zero
It was not a valid five-field expression.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Explain a business-hours schedule:
.PP
.RS
.B cron-translate '*/15 9-17 * * 1-5'
.RE
.PP
Next five runs in Rome, with DST warnings:
.PP
.RS
.B cron-translate '0 3 * * *' \-\-tz Europe/Rome \-\-next 5
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR crontab (5).
Project documentation:
.UR https://github.com/fabiocicerchia/cron-translate
.UE
